        <item>
            <title>1.5.19</title>
            <pubDate>Thu, 27 Aug 2026 19:37:09 +0900</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>51</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>1.5.19</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <description><![CDATA[<h3>Fixed</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>The tab and address bands are visible again on macOS 26 and later.</b> The new OS draws a glass "scroll edge" wash over the top of any pane the toolbar hands its own titlebar section, which blurred the tab strip and the address field — including the path editor — into an unreadable smear. Explorer no longer claims those sections, so both bands render normally. On macOS 26+ the toolbar's command block now sits at the window's leading edge and the redundant window title is hidden; the tab strip already names the current folder.</li>
<li><b>Going up now leaves the folder you came from selected.</b> The Up button, Go ▸ Enclosing Folder, and Backspace all reselect and scroll to the child folder in the parent listing, the way a breadcrumb click already did. Previously the selection was whatever happened to be highlighted last, so the folder stayed selected only by coincidence.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Added</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>35 mm equivalent focal length in the photo preview.</b> Photos that record it now show a "35 mm equiv." row beside the actual focal length. Phone photos often store only the equivalent, so those finally show a focal length at all. Full-frame bodies, where the two numbers agree, don't get a redundant second row.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Changed</h3>
<ul>
<li>The photo preview panel now lists <b>Size</b> and <b>Created</b> first, then the camera facts starting with <b>Dimensions</b>, so file information and photo information read as two separate blocks.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
